ABSD per-port patch for sbase 0.1 test (userland/README.md, "Patches").
Applies after test-access-mtime.patch.

Failure: the kit's ELF gate refuses the ABSD image of test with "C ELF
REFUSED: undefined symbol stdout (weak)". test writes only to stderr and
never names stdout, so Picolibc's stdout definition is not linked, and
Picolibc's buffered-I/O member (libc_stdio_bufio.c.o) references stdout
weakly; scripts/check-c-elf.py admits a weak undefined stdin only. Every
sbase utility that names stdout (all other ports here) links it.

Change: main() calls fflush(stdout) once before evaluating the expression,
so the program names stdout and links Picolibc's definition. Applied to
both builds (ABSD and the reference), so both run the same source.

Preserved: test writes nothing to stdout, so the flush has no data to write
and its result is not used; status and stderr are upstream's on both builds.
No adapter or kit change. (A gate that admits a weak stdout as it admits a
weak stdin would make this patch unnecessary; that is the kit's decision.)

--- a/test.c
+++ b/test.c
@@ -266,5 +266,11 @@
 	if (argc > 4)
 		enprintf(2, "too many arguments\n");
 
+	/* ABSD (userland/patches/test-stdout-gate.patch): name stdout, so that
+	   Picolibc's stream definition is linked and its buffered-I/O code's
+	   weak reference to it resolves (the kit's ELF gate refuses a weak
+	   undefined stdout). test writes nothing to stdout: the flush is empty. */
+	fflush(stdout);
+
 	return !narg[argc](argv);
 }
